            <p>In our rush toward convenience, audio quality is the one thing we’ve consistently sacrificed. The <strong>MP3 format</strong> revolutionized the music industry in the late 90s by shrinking massive CD files into something that could be shared over slow internet connections. But it did so by being "ruthless"—it removed frequencies that the human ear "typically" doesn't hear. While this sounds fine on basic earbuds, it strips away the "air" and "depth" of the music.</p>
            
